{"cuisine/transylvanian-saxon-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/transylvanian-saxon-cuisine","n":"Transylvanian Saxon cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Romanian cuisine","cuisine/romanian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Romania","intro":[["The Transylvanian Saxon cuisine is the traditional cuisine of the Transylvanian Saxons, a German ethnic group and minority which has been living in Transylvania as well as in other historical regions on the territory of present-day Romania since the mid-12th century onwards."],["It is a type of cuisine which, most notably, shares many similarities with Romanian cuisine and German cuisine, given the centuries-long mutual/reciprocal influence and cohabitation between the Saxons and Romanians in Transylvania, a Romanian historical region situated at the crossroads of Central, Eastern, and Southeastern Europe respectively. The Transylvanian Saxon cuisine has evolved since the High Middle Ages to the present day."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"transylvanian-saxon-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transylvanian_Saxon_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Romanian cuisine","cuisine/romanian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Romanian cuisine","cuisine/romanian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine"},"ingredient/biancaea-sappan":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/biancaea-sappan","n":"Biancaea sappan","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Biancaea sappan is a species of flowering tree in the legume family, Fabaceae, that is native to tropical Asia. Common names in English include sappan (after Malay sepang), sappanwood and Indian redwood.  It was previously ascribed to the genus Caesalpinia. Sappanwood is related to brazilwood (Paubrasilia echinata), and was itself called brasilwood in the Middle Ages."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-biancaea-sappan"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biancaea_sappan"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Betawi cuisine","cuisine/betawi-cuisine",[["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"]]],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cardamom","ingredient/cardamom"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Illicium verum","ingredient/illicium-verum"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Nigella sativa","ingredient/nigella-sativa"],["Pandan","ingredient/pandan"],["Piper retrofractum","ingredient/piper-retrofractum"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/32/Caesalpinia_sappan1.jpg/500px-Caesalpinia_sappan1.jpg","w":500,"h":452,"by":"Vinayaraj","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caesalpinia_sappan1.jpg"}},"dish/buchteln":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/buchteln","n":"Buchteln","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 3 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Buchteln are sweet rolls made of enriched yeast dough, filled with powidl, jam, ground poppy seeds or quark, brushed with butter and baked in a large pan so that they stick together and can be pulled apart. The traditional buchtel is filled with powidl. Buchteln may be topped with vanilla sauce, powdered sugar or eaten plain and warm. Buchteln are served tepid, mostly as a breakfast pastry or with tea. In the 19th century, they could be boiled similar to dumplings."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-buchteln"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buchteln"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Czech cuisine","cuisine/czech-cuisine"],["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine"],["Thuringian cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine-thuringia"]]},{"h":"Ingredie